      "notes": [
        "⚠️ **ნახევარი counts the half TOWARD the next hour, not past it.** *თერთმეტის ნახევარი* = \"half of eleven\" = **10:30**. German and Dutch do the same, while British \"half eleven\" means 11:30 — and this is the most common cause of foreigners arriving an hour late in Tbilisi. If in doubt, just say the numbers: *ათი ოცდაათი* (ten thirty) — always safe.",
        "**On-the-hour times: number + საათია.** *ხუთი საათია* (it's five o'clock). To make an appointment **at** a set time, add the ending **-ზე**: *ხუთ საათზე* (at five). Notice that ხუთ**ი** loses its -ი as soon as საათ**ზე** follows.",
        "**The days of the week are counted from Saturday.** შაბათი (Saturday, from the Hebrew *shabbat*) is the zero point, then: **ორ**შაბათი (two-Saturday = Monday), **სამ**შაბათი (three = Tuesday), **ოთხ**შაბათი (four = Wednesday), **ხუთ**შაბათი (five = Thursday). Friday has its own name: პარასკევი. If you know the numbers 2–5 from unit 6, you already know four day names.",
        "**კვირა has two meanings: \"Sunday\" and \"week\".** *კვირას* = on Sunday; *ერთი კვირა* = one week. Once again, context decides.",
        "**To say \"on …day\", use the ending -ს**: პარასკევ**ს** (on Friday), შაბათ**ს** (on Saturday), ორშაბათ**ს** (on Monday). This -ს ending is the one you'll meet in unit 9 as the object form.",
        "**დილით, საღამოს, ღამით — in the morning, in the evening, at night.** Notice that they take different endings, so it's best to learn each as a single word: დილ**ით** (in the morning), შუადღეს (at midday), საღამო**ს** (in the evening), ღამ**ით** (at night).",
        "**არ დაგავიწყდეს! = \"don't forget!\"** A whole sentence worth learning as is. The verb form is far beyond A1, but the expression is used every day."
      ],
